  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Write $18{c}^{2}+50$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$18{c}^{2}+50=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor out $2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n18{c}^{2}+50=2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{c}^{2}+25).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{c}^{2}+25 & =9{c}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & =9{c}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3c)^{2}-(5i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25)=2(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\n$$",
